As far as I'm concerned this game completes the circle. My first
nethack character back in 1990 was a tourist, and as we can see I have
*FINALLY* ascended one. This game is the very next game after my rogue
ascension -- this is a consecutive ascension, so I am now confident of
my ability to win a game with a class that can become expert in dagger.
Give me a stack of daggers and I will move the world, to mangle the
Greek philosopher :).

The game scared me right off when I started, because it decided to play
Santa Claus.  The first thing I saw on my first move was an altar right
next to me.   Going down through the maze, I found THREE altars, THREE
pairs of speed boots (!) ONE pair of boots of jumping, ONE Gauntlets of
Power, ONE shield of reflection and a wand of make invisible. And all
of this before I'd even reached the oracle.

I was frightened because it seemed as if I was being set up for a
horrible fall ... if the game was being this generous, how was it going
to balance out?  When would the other shoe drop?  And it did. Oh my,
did it ever.  But we're getting ahead of our story.  Scavenge armor,
moving from Banded mail to Dwarvish mithril to blue dragon scale mail
to silver dragon scale mail over the course of the game.

Sokoban had an amulet of reflection.   An altar sacrificing party
yielded Giantslayer.  "Use my gift wisely". Absolutely. Into the box
with you *CLONK*.

Turn 19492 -- For the first time in a long time I stumble into a poly
trap in mines end without magic resistance     and wind up as a gnome
zombie with 7 hit points.  I start throwing daggers into the air in
order to suicide this form ... and run out of energy. I don't even have
enough endurance to throw the dagger into the air.

There then follows a comedy of errors as the gnome king next to me
starts swinging and missing while I'm trying to drop enough stuff so I
have the strength to *throw a dagger in the air* and kill myself.
Eventually I succeed, revert to human form, kill the Gnome King, and
continue on through the maze.

I almost don't give the game a chance to drop the other shoe on it's
generosity by nearly killing myself twice.  The first time by engaging
a spotted jelly. I had 88 HP and after 2 rounds had 32.  WHOA!

So I stopped the fight and backed off.   Lesson: don't melee jellies.

The second time I nearly killed myself was in a soldier ant hive, where
I foolishly waded in slashing right and left and got knocked down to 32
HP before I came to my senses and ran away.  The game's first trick --
luring me into overconfidence with good equipment -- at this point
could be said to have failed, as I was VERY careful for the rest of the
game.

Perhaps TOO careful.  Twice over the next week I had nightmares in
which I was playing the game and my character was unaccountably stoned
or slimed  every ten moves or so. My subconscious had received the "be
careful" message with a vengeance.

Two Imperious orders from  thrones and some scrolls lying around in the
dungeon allow me to genocide Mind Flayers, Master Mind Flayers and L.

Master of Thieves presents no problem. Throw !paralysis at him but
miss.  Run back to the stairs dodging him until I'm standing on the
stairs, then kill him with daggers.  Acquire PYEC and unlimited
charging.

Receive Fire Brand from second sac party. Decide to keep it for now,
but to refrain from more sacrificing because I want Frost Brand and I
don't want to lower my chances of acquiring it any further.

At castle, use passtune to lower bridge. A fire giant steps out
followed by a cockatrice. I use a spetum to reach past the giant to
kill the cockatrice, then scoop it up and use it to clear out the front
half of the castle.

Eventually recover silver dragon scales which I use to make silver
dragon scale mail.

Wand of wishing contains 1 charge.  I use the wish, charge it and burn
two more, saving the last wish for emergencies.

I wish for:
Frost Brand
Ring of Teleport control
Blessed magic marker.

The game starts to pay me back for it's earlier generosity by locking
Fort Ludios. Scratch the money and tools I would otherwise have
acquired there.   I also carefully tin 3 tengu corpses to maximize my
chances of getting T-control. Then I eat a leprechaun corpse to get
Teleportitis, but none of the three tengu cans I eat gives me
T-control. I eventually wind up wishing for a ring of teleport control
-- the ONLY one I saw in the game -- to prevent uncontrolled jumps.

Use a scroll of taming to tame a purple worm. This works well until he
swallows a shapechanger and becomes an invisible stalker. He is
relatively useless and eventually died.

Buy off Baalzebub and Asmodeus.

Jubilex was a bit of a challenge -- I fell through a trap door onto his
level. I used Detect Treasure to find the stairs, and since it was
close, I pulled out a potion of extra healing and decided to make a run
for it.

I made within a step of the stairs when Jubilex engulfed me and made me
very sick indeed.

I tunnelled out of him, finished him off with a single blow of Frost
Brand, drank my extra healing potion ...

... and didn't get better.

This is how I learned that a cursed potion of extra healing does not
cure illness.

I swiftly pulled out a potion known to be blessed and saved my life,
but it was very close indeed.

I also learn what happens when you're swallowed by a doppleganger
polymorphed into a purple worm who changes shape again while you're
still inside him. I "broke out" of him and killed the now-barbarian
standing next to me.

Orcus was easy.  I simply walked up to him and bashed him with a potion
of paralysis.  Then I killed him with +5 Frost Brand and +5 silver
saber (later disenchanted to +3) .

Nearly die again by fainting from lack of food while nurse dancing.
Must pay attention to status line!

Meet two more purple worms.   I now know charm monster at 43% failure.
 We become ... distressingly intimate ... as they engulf me while I'm
trying to do the charm. It is eventually successful ... third time WAS
the charm, in each case ... they regurgitate me, and we continue into
the dungeon.  I eventually tame three purple worms this way.

Of these,  I lose two ... one to eating a shapechanger which changes it
into a homonculous. The other one becomes a green slime.  I abandon the
Homonculous and the green slime, because I do NOT want dozens of new
slimes throughout the dungeon.

Collect magical tools from all my caches, then polypile them to get ONE
magic marker. This serves to bring my protection down to -40.

Meanwhile,  my purple worm eats the oracle, to my disgust.

Down to acquire the book from Rodney. He goes down after one shot.
However, he later proves to be a jack in the box, and I kill him six
more times on the ascent.   Each time he takes one shot from a wand of
death ( I have collected two, including the one Orcus had, and recharge
them both multiple times using PYEC). Sometimes I miss the first time,
but get him the second time. He never inflicts serious damage.

Leave Burp the worm on the castle level. The reason being that A) he
will probably wind up abandoned on the planes, and the castle is a
better habitat B) Idon't want him getting hit by a stray death beam
when I'm fighting Rodney on the plane of earth, and costing me my
telepathy.

93452 -  Arrive Astral plane.
At first I intend to go left, but Pestilence comes at me from the right
and takes a death beam for his pains.  I seize the opportunity thus
afforded and charge right over his corpse.

Right altar is lawful. Darn.

Pestilence proves to be the Energizer bunny, and he eventually gets
killed 7 times before I finally get away.

Go up the center where the game finally pays me back for it's earlier
Santa Claus behavior.

Step up to the center temple.  There is a PC barbarian guarding it.
<Barbarian wields a greased long sword named Vorpal Blade!>

!!!!!
Great Scott! That's an instadeath-capable weapon! And I'm standing next
to him!
Needless to say, I jumped away in a hurry.

There then followed a careful ballet, as I tried to get a bead on him
with my wand of death... but every time I had a shot, the high priest
would step into line as well.  Killing a high priest in his own astral
temple was NOT on my list of things to do, so I carefully danced around
,waiting for my opportunity -- got one, but didn't realize that the
intervening creature between him and me was a silver dragon.  The beam
bounced.  Killed the dragon. Danced some more.  Finally got him lined
up. Fired. Got him! Hurried in and scooped up Vorpal Blade before
someone else could grab it.

Post-game identification reveals that I was facing off with a guy with
the +7 (!!!!) Vorpal blade.   That is just unfair -- except, of course,
neither is three pairs of speed boots, a gauntlets of power, a shield
of reflection and a wand of make invisible. Once again the law of large
numbers smooths the edges out.

It was a reminder in a most clear way that, as it says in Pilgrim's
Progress, there is a door to Hell even at the very gates of Heaven.  In
Nethack, anyway.

Lesson for me; When playing neutral character, make certain I get
crowned before attempting the run, So I can be sure of having Vorpal
blade and either keep it or throw it in a box.

Anyway, I make it past the barbarian to the center altar.  This altar
turns out to be chaotic. Double darn.

Death guards the center passage, and I learn a lot about jumping from
this experience.

I learn you can't jump while burdened.  *You carry too much to jump*
I learn you can't jump over a giant beetle *Ouch! You hit a giant
beetle!*

The tuition for this learning was 73 hit points permanently sucked away
from me by death, knocking me from 300 HP to 227.  I killed him once
with throwing daggers, killed him a second time from melee when I lost
patience, and the third time just jumped away from him and ran all the
way, knocking pestilence down a 7th time on the way, and headed left.

Famine was waiting at the left-hand door.   Zapped him down with the
almost last charge in my drained wands of death.  A few more jumps get
me past him to the left altar -- which is indeed the correct altar,
although I double check just to be sure. I haven't come all this way to
leave Heaven in celestial disgrace!

Step up to altar. Burn my last wish in my wand of wishing for "100
dilithium crystals" to pump up my end score. I get one. Next time I'll
wish for gold.

Turn 93743 YAAP.

